AB  - This study investigates the primary challenges hindering the progress of smart agriculture, including “rising costs, significant technical barriers, and extensive resource consumption,” as well as “challenges in promotion, training, and implementation.” It suggests three main parts for a “Smart Agriculture Exhibition Hall” virtual simulation system that combines generative AI with virtual reality: (1) Creating an intelligent generation toolchain for agriculture that uses multimodal large-model technology to make it easier to do high-precision dynamic simulations of crop growth, digital twin modeling of agricultural machinery, and realistic simulations of agricultural environments. (2) Setting up a multi-level virtual promotion space to create a point-to-surface agricultural technology collaboration network that goes beyond geographical barriers, which will improve knowledge sharing and dissemination. (3) Designing a four-dimensional cultivation system of “cognition-observation-practice-communication” to create an immersive and interactive closed loop for agricultural technology training effectively improves training quality (effect size d > 1.2) and reduces training costs (40% efficiency improvement). This study goes beyond the limits of static modeling in traditional agricultural simulation by providing a new optimization method called “generation-simulation-evaluation.” It provides smart agriculture with a reusable technical framework for digital transformation. © 2025 IEEE.
