TI  - Beyond Potemkin Understanding: Towards Coherent World Models in Video AI
PY  - 2025
T2  - Proceedings of the International Workshop on Intelligent Immersification in the Metaverse: AI-Driven Immersive Multimedia
SP  - 55
PB  - Association for Computing Machinery
DO  - 10.1145/3728487.3759219
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1145/3728487.3759219
AB  - Contemporary foundation models often display 'Potemkin understanding': fluent outputs resting on brittle, locally trained correlations. The notion of a world model serves here not as a ready-made remedy but as a conceptual lens that reveals what is missing—temporally coherent, causally grounded representations of the processes that generate data. Nowhere is this deficit clearer than in video generation: adjacent frames contribute scant new signal, so scale alone rarely yields agents that respect long-range dynamics or latent physics, unlike information-dense next-token prediction in language modelling. Entering an era of experience therefore requires training protocols that compel models to act, predict and revise across extended horizons, with principles distilled from humanity's scientific drive to compress reality informing architecture and objective design. These directions, rather than the world-model label itself, point towards more credible AI avatars and immersive XR systems whose behaviour remains consistent well beyond benchmark snippets.
