AB  - Recent advancements in large language models (LLMs) have shown promise for human-like machine intelligence. Enabling these models in wearable augmented reality (AR) devices can elevate the user experiences with reduced latency and improved privacy, seamlessly blending advanced machine intelligence (artificial general intelligence) into daily life. However, deploying these models on wearable AR devices is hindered by the limited memory capacity and high energy/latency costs associated with off-chip memory access. This article assesses the recent memory technology innovations, including 3D-stacked memories, computing-in-memory (CIM), and processing-in-memory (PIM), and it explores how to utilize their potential to enable LLMs on AR SoCs. Using roofline modeling, we showcase the importance of cooptimizing system architecture with model characteristics to meet power/performance targets. © 2009-2012 IEEE.
