TI  - The Impact of Collaborative Generative Learning Activities in Virtual Reality Safety Training
PY  - 2025
T2  - Computing in Civil Engineering 2025: Resilient, Robotic, and Educational Systems - Selected Papers from the ASCE International Conference on Computing in Civil Engineering 2025
SP  - 1013
EP  - 1018
DO  - 10.1061/9780784486443.111
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105031010148?origin=resultslist
AB  - Virtual reality (VR) is an essential tool in construction safety training, allowing the simulation of real-world activities without exposing workers to risks. Conventional VR-based training often relies on repetitive scenarios, limiting worker engagement and analytical skills. This study proposes a multi-user VR safety training system integrating generative and collaborative learning. The system includes 10 simulated hazard scenarios addressing key hazards, provides real-time video feedback to analyze mistakes, and reinforces safety knowledge. A laboratory experiment was conducted to compare collaborative generative learning with two other approaches: (1) passive learning, and (2) individual generative learning. Results showed that collaborative generative learning activities significantly improved safety knowledge, hazard perception, and detection skills, surpassing other approaches. Individual generative learning activities was also more effective than passive learning by increasing engagement and hazard detection. These findings underscore the potential of multi-user VR safety training to improve safety outcomes and reduce risks in dynamic construction environments. © ASCE.
