AB  - The development of artificial intelligence (AI) and augmented reality (AR) has significantly revolutionized the fashion industry, as it concerns personalization, sustainability, and virtual shopping experiences. The traditional fashion production and online shopping systems often use fixed representations of products and physical testing, thus causing the increase of material waste, ineffective use of resources, and the increase of the products' returns. These limitations result in the need to explore smart digital capabilities that can be used to improve visualization, responsible production, and consumer decision-making in online fashion stores. This study proposes a consistent fashion design system that incorporates diffusion-based generative AI into a modularized client-server system to help in the initial design and visualization of clothes. The system also allows users to assign garment properties such as type, fabric, color, and stylistic preference, which are stochastically converted into formatted natural language, which in turn allows a diffusion-based model to generate high-quality fashion design images without the need to have local models trained, thereby reducing computational expenses and increasing access to the system. The experimental research design was a prototype-based research study to determine the viability and functionality of the system. Sixty different design prompts were tested, resulting in a 100% request completion rate and an average response period of 21.4 seconds, which was not affected by the variations of input complexities. Quantitative analysis proved the statistically significant correspondence between inputs introduced by users and the output provided by the systems, and the qualitative analysis revealed a coherent aesthetic and design diversity, which made sense throughout the generated images. Overall, the results reveal that the diffusion-based generative AI has been successfully tested in terms of the ability to convert textual fashion specifications into the visually consistent design ideas, allowing exploration of the design space fast and avoiding the dependence on physical sampling. The suggested framework has significant potential for increasing the level of personalization, efficiency, and sustainability within digital fashion platforms and can be applied to other fashion goods and digital shopping settings. © 2026 IEEE.
