AB  - Mixed reality technology is increasingly used in cultural heritage guided tours, and the integration of large language models enables visitors to obtain personalized interpretations of artworks through natural language. However, the autoregressive generation mechanism of LLMs introduces multi-second response latency, requiring visitors to wait extended periods after posing a question before receiving an answer, thereby disrupting the immersive experience. Existing studies mitigate perceived latency through conversational filler strategies, yet provide no questionrelevant information during the waiting period. This paper proposes a dual-model asynchronous generation architecture. An edge-deployed small model generates a concise summary by retrieving and rewriting knowledge entries, while a cloudbased large model simultaneously produces detailed content organized by dimensions in the background. Visitors see the summary immediately after asking a question and can then expand dimensions of interest on demand to access detailed information. The time spent reading the summary overlaps temporally with the large model's generation process, making the expansion operation near-instantaneous. Experiments show that the waiting time from question submission to the first meaningful response is reduced from 4.48 seconds to 0.91 seconds, falling below the 4-second threshold for experience quality degradation reported in prior research. Visitors have content available to read throughout the entire interaction process, eliminating blank waiting periods and improving the fluency and continuity of the guided tour experience. © 2026 IEEE.
