TI  - A Study on the Game between Generative AI and Human Cognitive Thinking
PY  - 2026
T2  - Proceedings of the 2nd International Conference on Artificial Intelligence, Virtual Reality and Interaction Design
SP  - 1394
EP  - 1399
PB  - Association for Computing Machinery
DO  - 10.1145/3820915.3821132
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1145/3820915.3821132
AB  - With the rapid iteration and popularization of computer technologies such as generative artificial intelligence, multimodal interaction, natural language processing, computer vision, and large-scale pre-trained models, artificial intelligence has deeply penetrated the core scenarios of human cognitive activities, and gradually formed a multi-dimensional and in-depth game relationship with human cognitive thinking. This game is not a simple compromise and confrontation, but a dynamic interaction and tension balance centered on key links such as information filtering, demand satisfaction, and cognitive judgment. In essence, it is the collision and adaptation between artificial intelligence algorithm logic and computational models, as well as human cognitive laws and value preferences. From the interdisciplinary perspective of cognitive psychology, artificial intelligence ethics and game theory, this paper follows the core research logic of “phenomenon analysis – mechanism interpretation – quantitative characterization – conclusion condensation”. It systematically analyzes the specific forms of the game between artificial intelligence and human cognitive thinking, focusing on three core game phenomena: cognitive competition, cognitive pressure and cognitive bias. A formula for cognitive resource occupancy is introduced to achieve quantitative characterization, and three groups of engineering charts are combined to intuitively present the game characteristics. The paper further interprets two major balancing mechanisms in the current game relationship: asymmetric narration and dynamic game. Finally, it condenses research conclusions and puts forward prospects for future studies, so as to provide solid theoretical support and operable practical references for regulating the application of artificial intelligence technology, safeguarding human cognitive subjectivity, and realizing the high-quality development of human-machine cognitive collaboration.
