AB  - Educational AI may function as a teammate when responses remain tightly tied to what learners are doing. We analyze context-grounded conversational support for immersive algebra activity in VR, where learner intent must be inferred from speech, gestures, object state, and recent manipulation history. The system compiles this interaction state into each model turn, and we evaluate the design on 30 scripted traces using full-context and ablated conditions. Our evaluation prioritizes teammate behavior rather than generic fluency, focusing on action grounding, collaborative usefulness, and failure patterns. With full context enabled, action-reference accuracy rises from 38% to 92%, relevance from 2.9 to 4.6 (0–5), and pedagogical value from 2.7 to 4.3, while hallucination events drop from 14 to 2. Response time increases modestly (1.21 s to 1.38 s). We discuss how interaction-state conditioning may make educational LLM support more dependable in this setting, while emphasizing that these results evaluate interaction quality rather than learning gains. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.
