AB  - This paper presents a novel controllable generative framework for intelligent furniture design rendering and style transfer, integrating a U-Net backbone with the Stable Difusion model. By leveraging multi-modal inputs including sketches, text prompts, and style reference images, the framework achieves precise structural control and high-fidelity style migration in furniture efect maps. The U-Net encoder extracts multi-scale control signals (edges, depth, layout) that are injected into the difusion denoising process, while a lightweight LoRA adapter enables efficient cross-style transfer with minimal parameter overhead. Trained in two stages—first for controllable generation and second for style adaptation—the model demonstrates superior performance over baselines, with FID reduced to 8.3, CLIP score reaching 0.94, and SSIM at 0.92. Extensive experiments on public furniture sketch and interior style datasets validate the framework's efectiveness in real-world design scenarios, ofering designers an automated, editable pipeline for rapid prototyping. The approach bridges traditional CAD limitations with generative AI, providing a scalable solution for the furniture industry. Ablation studies confirm the necessity of each component, and qualitative results showcase photorealistic outputs across diverse styles. This work advances generative AI applications in industrial design, with potential extensions to 3D modeling and virtual reality integration.
