AB  - In rapidly evolving virtual environments involving human, robotic and AI-based agents, ethical oversight becomes increasingly complex. Traditional frameworks often treat non-human agents as tools, overlooking the relational and distributed nature of influence in multi-agent systems. This paper applies the Good Enough Ethics (GEE) framework, developed through interdisciplinary dialogue and real-world technological collaboration, as a responsive alternative to checklist or perfectionist models. Centred on six relational principles - attachment, trust, autonomy, privacy, justice and responsibility - GEE embraces embedded, iterative and co-designed ethical practice. Our central questions - Who is ethics for? and Should the same ethical principles apply across all agents? - guide two case studies: a qualitative study of the SHARESPACE XR platform and a speculative application to Large Language Models (LLMs). We explore how ethics can be manipulated as performance, and how GEE enables a differentiated, pragmatic approach to ethics across varied agent types. © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).
