TI  - Digital Twin framework for a Smart Hydrogen Energy District: Real-time monitoring and probabilistic predictive analytics
PY  - 2026
T2  - Computers and Industrial Engineering
VL  - 222
DO  - 10.1016/j.cie.2026.112372
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105050717515?origin=resultslist
AB  - We report on the design and implementation of a unified Digital Twin (DT) framework for a Smart Hydrogen Energy District (SHED). The framework is designed to overcome the limitations of high-frequency data streams which can create cognitive overload for operators. This SHED-DT enables real-time monitoring, immersive user-interface interactions, virtual exploration, and probabilistic predictive analytics for the integrated energy system. The framework integrates sensor data from multiple sources, and it enables an interactive navigation platform for operators, as well as concurrent monitoring of live performance metrics. The back-end synchronizes and cleans over a thousand signals with a mean latency of 2.5 s and greater than 99.9% completeness, while the front-end renders a live, navigable 3D DT of the district. The DT's functionality includes probabilistic, time-series models that process both historical and real-time data to predict energy production and demand. The integration of probabilistic model forecasting improves reliability compared with traditional deterministic methods. The forecasting model achieves a mean continuous ranked probability score (CRPS) of 0.13, which reduces forecasting errors by more than 50% relative to a deterministic baseline. An ablation study shows that the immersive 3D interface can reduce operator reaction time by approximately 40% compared to conventional 2D dashboards. In combination with high-fidelity immersive visualization, the DT supports operators with real-time decision making and enables the management of advanced energy districts. The platform is demonstrated with a case study that highlights its role in improving efficiency, reliability, and enabling energy visualization and planning. Our preliminary findings demonstrate that the platform improves situational awareness and reduces operator reaction times. This work bridges the gap between physical assets and cyber–physical decision support systems. © 2026
