AB  - Intelligent transformation in fashion design increasingly depends on the integration of generative artificial intelligence, virtual prototyping, and craft-aware material development. Yet for woolen garments and crochet-based womenswear, the workflow from concept generation to manufacturable structure is still often fragmented: visual ideation is rapid, but material logic, structural translation, and physical validation remain slow and highly manual. This paper proposes a Digital Workflow for Intelligent Woolen Garment Development (DWIWD) that combines craft-material knowledge capture, AI-assisted concept expansion, 3D virtual prototyping, and physical sampling in a closed-loop process. The workflow is validated through the womenswear case Qianmo, which integrates crochet structures and Tyvek elements inspired by fish scales and water gradients. The case is suitable for intelligent woolen design because it contains both soft yarn logic and semi-rigid modular components, making it an effective testbed for digital translation, virtual verification, and craft refinement. Based on retrospective process records reconstructed from the project documentation, the proposed workflow reduced downstream physical sample rounds from five to two and shortened total iteration time from 41.5 h to 26.0 h. To formalize decision control, the paper introduces a Digital Feasibility Index that screens candidate solutions before sampling. The study contributes a reproducible, engineering-oriented workflow for woolen apparel development, demonstrating how AI and virtual prototyping can augment rather than replace craft intelligence.
