AB  - While generative AI promises to revolutionize creative fields like interior design, its unstructured application frequently leads to conceptually disjointed or stylistically homogeneous results. To address this challenge, we introduce and evaluate an AI-assisted framework that operationalizes the Concept-Motive-Style (CMS) model. Our Dify-based AI agent systematically guides users through concept development, symbolic motive extraction, and style selection, leveraging real-time web resources and text-to-image generation to impose methodological rigor. In a preliminary comparative study (N=10 undergraduates), our agent was assessed against a baseline unstructured tool. Subjective assessments indicated high user satisfaction with the framework's ability to enhance process efficiency and conceptual clarity. These findings were corroborated by objective metrics revealing statistically significant improvements in image realism and perceptual quality (FID, BRISQUE; p < .05) over the baseline. However, a critical discrepancy emerged: user satisfaction with the final visualization quality was markedly lower, and gains in image diversity (IS) did not reach statistical significance. These results suggest that while a structured, theory-driven approach effectively improves conceptual coherence, a bottleneck persists in translating design intent into high-fidelity visual outputs. The study's conclusions are limited by its preliminary design, small student sample, and absence of a professional control group. Nevertheless, this work provides a crucial empirical foundation for developing structured human-AI co-creation methodologies and highlights the central challenge of balancing procedural rigor with generative quality.
