AB  - Co-located Extended Reality (XR) installations increasingly seek to support social and cooperative play. However groups can struggle to coordinate and interpret cues to act, which inhibits their participation in the experience of the installation. My practice-based PhD thus investigates how nonverbal sensorial feedback, driven by real-time data and mediated by generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) facilitators, might support pro-social and co-creative group play in room-scale co-located social XR installations. Developed in Unreal Engine 5, my extended reality (XR) installation integrates Azure Kinect Sensors to TouchDesigner and streams real-time data via Open Sound Control (OSC), which feeds into the GenAI facilitation layer. The GenAI facilitation layer parses the data and returns cue actions that Unreal enacts as a world-anchored audiovisual intervention that the groups can then coordinate and interpret. Through convergent mixed-methods analysis, I examine how groups interpret cues, how norms emerge during play, and which design configurations help scaffold cooperative pro-social play. The outcome of my PhD is an emerging evidence-led Social XR Framework that offers reusable design patterns for nonverbal GenAI Facilitation and my installation projects as research artefacts that can be reproduced in different contexts by other researchers. © 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).
