AB  - The deep integration of generative artificial intelligence (AI) is reshaping the paradigm of artistic creation, transforming “human-AI co-creation” from a technological possibility into a critical issue that art education must address. Based on theoretical reasoning and literature synthesis, this study systematically reviews cutting-edge theories of human-AI co-creativity and constructs a five-stage co-creative model encompassing “Divergent Exploration - Intent Articulation - Co-generative Exploration - Critical Curation - Iterative Refinement.” Integrating Jeongki Lim's theory of “relational artifacts,” Duri Long's framework of “co-creative AI,” and recent systematic review findings, the study elucidates the intrinsic mechanisms of human-AI co-creation from the dual perspectives of relational construction and interaction design. The findings reveal that human-AI co-creation is fundamentally a deep collaborative relationship characterized by “human-led, AI-enhanced” dynamics: AI expands the possibility space of creativity, while humans remain the ultimate agents of intention setting, value judgment, and meaning construction. The “imperfections” of AI-generated images are transformed into creative resources through mechanisms of “serendipity capture,” “critical awareness against over-compliance,” and “metacognitive reflection in intention-output gaps.” This transformation is reshaping the value structure of art education, shifting it from an exclusive focus on “skill training” toward the multidimensional integration of “creative cultivation,” “narrative capacity,” and “aesthetic competence.” Based on these findings, this paper proposes collaborative teaching strategies for future-oriented art education.
