AB  - Financial text classification benchmarking has been widely used in quantitative trading systems development, and typical tasks involve financial sentiment classification and classification of financial news. Here, we look to the more recent application of advanced natural language processing (NLP) techniques. This study looks at the performance of the DeepSeek model (a large language model with 7B parameters) on both tasks. As stock and futures markets continue to grow in complexity, quantitative trading systems can not only process price data of financial products, but must also react in real-time to news and investor sentiment.In this research, we finetuned the DeepSeek Model using Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) on a Twitter based financial news dataset and financial sentiment classification dataset. We also implemented FlashAttention to speed up training time and use less GPU memory. In our experiments, we compared DeepSeek's performance against those of traditional non-LLM models including RoBERTa, DeBERTa and T5 and also large language models such as LLaMA2 and Baichuan2. The results indicated that DeepSeek outperformed the traditional models and the LLMs even with fewer training iterations. The results showed the benefits of the DeepSeek approach, as it produced better classification results with less data and fewer training epochs, thus overall providing a solid basis for future ethical and widely scalable applications of NLP in the media space.
