AB  - Smart home technology is changing quickly, and this has helped us understand the problems with current designs, such as fragmented system controls, lack of context, and static control which can't adapt to different user settings and preferences. This study analyses at recent developments in three areas that overlap: augmented reality user interfaces, multimodal communication strategies, and AI-based personalisation. We look at 20 of these papers from the year 2022 to 2025 to find the most important ones and the problems that affect these fields. AR-based interfaces are 30-40% quicker than smartphone interfaces, and gesture recognition techniques that use the MediaPipe system are 88-95% accurate in the best conditions. Even with these changes, present systems are still limited by fixed learning models, sensitivity to the environment, and limited integration across domains. To solve these problems, we suggest a single framework that combines augmented reality overlays with spatial context, multiple modes of voice and gesture interaction for natural management, and a generative computing intelligent Multi-Armed Bandit (GenMAB) system for adaptive automation. GenMAB combines positive reinforcement training for making the best decisions with generative modelling for context-aware exploration. This lets you keep personalising without having to retrain the whole model. Our results show that user satisfaction and availability have improved significantly, energy optimisation has increased by 10-30%, and habit-tracking adherence has increased by more than 80%. This research not only provides a roadmap for the development of future-proof autonomous houses that are readily available, adaptable, and genuinely intelligent, but it also offers a thorough comprehension of current research trajectories. © 2026 IEEE.
