AB  - The increasing prevalence of artificial intelligence (AI) in design practice has sparked interest in AI-assisted creativity; however, existing research focuses on technical performance or visual effects, with little attention paid to how AI reshapes design processes in craft-based design fields. This study aims to fill this gap by exploring how AI-assisted tools transform the ceramic product design workflow from a process-oriented perspective. Using a qualitative, practice-based approach, this study reconstructs and compares traditional and AI-assisted ceramic product design workflows through design experiments and protocol analysis. The results show that AI primarily intervenes in the early ideation stage of the design process, introducing a generative layer, reorganizing iterative patterns, and front-loading the exploration process. Rather than replacing traditional practices, AI reshapes the workflow structure, shifting the designer's role from direct generation to curation, evaluation, and contextual judgment, while material experimentation, craft knowledge, and cultural understanding remain crucial in the later stages of the design process. This study contributes to design research by providing empirical evidence from a craft-based design field and proposing a workflow reconstruction method to facilitate human-AI collaboration. By positioning AI as a workflow intermediary embedded within design practice, this study offers a deeper understanding of how AI supports innovation in ceramic product design while maintaining cultural continuity and craft integrity.
