TI  - Reliability of Edge AI in XR-Based Health Data Capture: An Experimental Study on GPU Offloading and Semantic Stability
PY  - 2026
T2  - IEEE Access
VL  - 14
SP  - 68315
EP  - 68333
DO  - 10.1109/ACCESS.2026.3689595
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105037884181?origin=resultslist
AB  - Edge artificial intelligence (AI) is increasingly required in extended reality (XR) healthcare systems to characterize latency behavior under controlled execution constraints, energy efficiency, and privacy-preserving processing of sensitive patient data. This paper presents an edge-native multimodal XR architecture for structured health data capture, integrating automatic speech recognition, quantized large language model inference, and deterministic adaptation within a fully on-device pipeline. We conduct a controlled experimental evaluation of heterogeneous graphics processing unit (GPU) offloading levels (GPU Layers = 0, 20, 30), with n = 30 runs per configuration, and quantify latency-energy scaling under distinct embedded power modes (7 W and 15 W). Beyond conventional performance metrics, we introduce semantic stability indicators for structured JSON-based clinical extraction, including schema validity, completion rate, and critical-field null rate. Results reveal a systematic latency-semantic trade-off: aggressive GPU offloading reduces end-to-end latency but may induce complete loss of critical structured fields under specific configurations, reaching a 100% null rate in extreme cases. The findings on the evaluated platform demonstrate that edge optimization strategies in XR healthcare pipelines should be assessed not only in terms of computational efficiency but also in terms of semantic reliability. The proposed architecture and evaluation framework provide experimentally reproducible characterization for designing trustworthy edge AI systems in real-time clinical XR environments. © 2026 The Authors.
