SP  - 283
EP  - 289
PB  - Association for Computing Machinery
DO  - 10.1145/3820915.3820958
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1145/3820915.3820958
AB  - With the advancement of generative AI technology, its applications in visual design have garnered significant attention. To address challenges in traditional regional cultural visual communication—including limited design efficiency, insufficient creative expression, and difficulties in translating cultural symbols—this study focuses on Bashu regional culture and establishes a cultural visual design research framework based on AIGC (ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE GENERATED CONTENT). The research first systematically collects visual resources of Bashu culture, creating a cultural visual database that includes traditional patterns, cultural symbols, performing arts, and architectural elements. Subsequently, through cultural feature extraction and PROMPT prompt design, generative AI models generate cultural visual images, which are then integrated via a collaborative “AI generation + designer optimization” model. During the experimental phase, the AI model generated 120 visual design proposals across three categories: cultural posters, product packaging, and cultural illustrations. Thirty design professionals were invited to evaluate the proposals, and a five-point Likert scale was used to construct a cultural visual communication evaluation system. Results showed that all metrics averaged over 4.0, with the visual creativity index reaching 4.47, indicating strong performance in creative expression and cultural symbol presentation. Compared to traditional design processes, AI-assisted design improved creativity scores by approximately 16% and significantly enhanced design efficiency. The research findings demonstrate that AIGC technology can effectively expand the creative space for cultural visual design, while providing innovative design methodologies and practical approaches for regional cultural visual communication.
