TI  - Research on design of interactive entertainment system for second language acquisition based on Speech-Gesture multimodal perception
PY  - 2026
T2  - Entertainment Computing
VL  - 58
DO  - 10.1016/j.entcom.2026.101152
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105040926125?origin=resultslist
AB  - Traditional second language acquisition tools have a single interaction, a lack of immersion and self-adaptation ability, and are difficult to stimulate learning motivation. This study designs and implements a second language acquisition interactive entertainment system integrating voice-gesture multimodal perception, integrating an Extended Reality (XR) immersive environment, and an intelligent interaction mechanism. The system architecture includes voice emotion recognition, gesture motion capture, and a multi-modal feature cascade fusion algorithm, and adopts generative Artificial Intelligence (AI) to dynamically adjust the feedback and narrative direction of virtual characters to realize content adaptation based on user emotional state. In order to verify the effectiveness of the system, 30 s-language learners were recruited to carry out a comparative experiment: the experimental group used this system to complete interactive tasks in XR scenes, while the control group used a traditional APP to learn. Assessment indicators include multimodal recognition accuracy, learning effect (vocabulary test), user experience (immersion questionnaire), and emotional adaptability. The results show that the recognition rate of multi-modal fusion reaches 94.3%, which is 12.7% higher than that of single modal fusion; The average post-test score of vocabulary in the experimental group was 85.6 (72.4 in the control group, p < 0.01); The user experience immersion score is 4.6/5; The matching accuracy of emotional feedback and user status was 91.2%. The innovations of this study are as follows: 1) Proposing a speech-gesture collaborative perception framework to enhance interaction robustness through spatiotemporal alignment; 2) Building an emotion-driven adaptive feedback mechanism to dynamically optimize learning content and role behavior; 3) Designing XR immersive second language acquisition scenarios, integrating generative narrative and multi-modal interaction, and enhancing learning motivation and entertainment experience. This study provides empirical evidence supporting the effectiveness of multimodal intelligent interaction in enhancing learning performance and user experience within XR-based environments. © 2026 Elsevier B.V. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.
