AB  - With the growing demand for digital preservation and dissemination of cultural heritage, traditional exhibition methods have increasingly revealed limitations in terms of immersion, interactivity, and narrative depth. To enhance the effectiveness of cultural heritage communication, this study investigates immersive exhibition approaches that integrate extended reality (XR) with digital storytelling, establishing a multi-tiered immersive display system tailored for cultural heritage contexts. The system utilizes cultural heritage data to digitally reconstruct historical scenes through 3D reconstruction technology, combined with XR rendering engines to create immersive spatial environments. It incorporates digital storytelling mechanisms-including timeline narratives, character-driven narratives, and scenario-based storytelling-to dynamically present cultural content, while employing multimodal interaction technologies to enhance user engagement. Furthermore, the study develops a user experience evaluation model encompassing immersion, interactivity, emotional resonance, and cultural comprehension, analyzing system performance through multidimensional metrics. Results demonstrate that this methodology significantly improves user immersion experiences and cultural understanding, strengthening emotional connections and memory retention in cultural communication. This work establishes a novel paradigm integrating spatial computing with narrative design for digital cultural heritage exhibitions, providing theoretical and methodological support for the development of smart museums and digital cultural tourism.
