AB  - The growing demand for scalable and effective professional education has driven innovation in the application of immersive technologies, notably Extended Reality (XR) and Artificial Intelligence (AI). This paper investigates the progressive evolution of educational XR applications across three domain-specific use cases: VoltXR for electrical apprentices, StressVR for healthcare professionals, and ClassroomVR for student teachers. Drawing on empirical data and design documentation, we explore how requirements have shifted from procedural training to emotionally intelligent, user-adaptive learning environments. We analyze changes in pedagogical objectives, technological affordances, integration complexity, and user autonomy. This comparative study outlines the trajectory from foundational skill acquisition to the cultivation of socio-emotional competencies and offers insights into the implications for future XR development in vocational and higher education. © ISES 2026.
