AB  - XR based learning environments are increasingly being integrated with LLMs to provide enhanced learning experience to learners. This study investigates how students engage with a smartphone-based XR–LLM phygital learning system for academic reading. Specifically, we analyzed learners transitions between printed textbook and smartphone application, and the nature of inquiry with LLM. Using Transition Network Analysis (TNA) on multimodal log data of (N = 50) grade ninth standard students in India, which includes trace data and LLM interactions (prompts and response), we examine the temporal patterns of learner behavior in an academic reading task. Additionally, we report differences in learners’ LLM inquiry strategies, and explore their relationships with comprehension and workload indicators. This work contributes to understanding how learners orchestrate LLM-assisted reading in an XR-LLM phygital learning system. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.
